# Line endings.
#
# core.autocrlf=true on Windows checks these out with CRLF, which makes every
# formatter that reads the working tree report a violation on every file. Measured
# 2026-08-07: `gofmt -l` over the tracked Go files reported 678 of 925; against the
# LF content in the index the real number was 26. A gate the operator cannot
# reproduce on their own machine is its own defect class, so the tools that CI runs
# must see the same bytes locally.
#
# Anything a formatter, linter or diff-based gate reads is pinned to LF here.

*.go   text eol=lf
*.mod  text eol=lf
*.sum  text eol=lf

*.ts   text eol=lf
*.tsx  text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.jsx   text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.cjs   text eol=lf

# JSON is global, and unlike the markdown rule below it is NOT pinned by path.
# Raised on PR #21: `.mnfs/`, the provider `testdata/` trees and
# `docs/research/evidence/` hold recorded output, and `.prettierignore` argues at
# length that a tool rewriting recorded bytes destroys the record -- a line
# ending is still a byte. The two rules say opposite things about the same files.
#
# Measured 2026-08-08 before deciding: of the 68 JSON files across those three
# trees, 0 are CRLF in the index. This attribute rewrites nothing today; the
# exposure is a future capture that arrives with CRLF and is normalised on the
# way in, silently.
#
# Left global on purpose. The only attribute that would actually guarantee
# verbatim bytes is `-text`, and that also marks all 68 binary in `git diff` --
# for `.mnfs/` that is cheap, for a recorded provider payload under `testdata/`
# it is not, because the diff of what the API returned is the point of keeping
# it. Paid when a capture is measured to have been altered, not before.
*.json  text eol=lf

*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

*.sh text eol=lf

# Markdown, pinned by path rather than globally.
#
# Prettier checks markdown, and with `endOfLine: lf` a CRLF checkout fails every
# file. Measured 2026-08-08: 30 of the 251 markdown files in Prettier's scope are
# CRLF in the working tree.
#
# Pinned per directory rather than as `*.md`, so `.mnfs/` is never touched. That
# tree holds 1000 markdown files of mission evidence, and renormalising them
# rewrites their stored bytes -- a line-ending change is still a change to a
# record that is supposed to be verbatim. Exempting it with `-text` would work
# and would also mark every one of them binary in `git diff`, which costs more
# than it buys. Absence is the exemption.
/*.md           text eol=lf
docs/**/*.md    text eol=lf
wiki/**/*.md    text eol=lf
contracts/**/*.md      text eol=lf
design-system/**/*.md  text eol=lf

# Consumed by Caddy inside a Linux container, and copied to the production host
# from a checkout. A CRLF checkout on Windows would be the config the operator
# ships without ever having run it that way.
Caddyfile text eol=lf
*.Caddyfile text eol=lf
